  "text": "Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisementHealthIs This the Hidden Cause of Allergies?It may not start with the immune system after allJCBy Jake Currie6:00 PM CST on November 20, 2026Add Nautilus to GoogleAllergies are on the rise. According to government statistics, cases of eczema, hay fever, and food allergies have been steadily climbing in the United States over the past few decades, leaving researchers puzzled. A slew of hypotheses for the increase have been offered—from too much hygiene to not enough gut microbes. Now, a new study published in the journal Allergy suggests our understanding of the illnesses may be flawed from the beginning.  Powered By 10 ShareNextStayAllergies have traditionally been understood as an exaggerated response from an oversensitive immune system. A harmless allergen triggers a cascade of immune activity causing sneezes, inflammation, and in extreme cases even death. On a tissue level, part of the damage done by allergies has been to epithelial barrier cells lining the gut, skin, airways, and more. But what if we have things backwards?Read more: “The Perils of Early Springtime”Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisement",
